# Local overrides for running scripts/simulate_incidents.py by hand.
# Copy to .env (gitignored) and fill in. CI does NOT read this file -- the workflow
# passes these as real environment variables, which always win over anything here.
#
# Every one of these is optional. With SENTRY_DSN blank, observability.py no-ops and
# the failure paths still run, which is the quickest way to check the simulator
# survives a change to the code it exercises.

# Project -> Settings -> Client Keys (DSN). Blank = report nothing.
#
# !! Leave this BLANK unless you mean it. An issue's `firstRelease` is fixed the
# first time Sentry ever sees it and is never recalculated. Run the simulator
# locally against a real DSN before CI has run, and all three issues are created
# with your local release (or none at all) -- so when CI later emits them under the
# merge commit SHA, `firstRelease:<merge sha>` matches nothing and Provenance shows
# the PR as having caused no incidents. Silently, with no error anywhere.
#
# If that has already happened: delete those issues in Sentry (Issues -> select ->
# Delete) and re-run the workflow, which recreates them against the right release.
SENTRY_DSN=

# The release to file events against. MUST be the merge commit SHA for Provenance to
# join these incidents back to a PR -- see the header of observability.py. Locally:
#   SENTRY_RELEASE=$(git rev-parse HEAD)
# In CI the workflow sets this to github.sha and this file is ignored entirely.
SENTRY_RELEASE=

# Defaults to "development" when unset. CI uses "production".
SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=
